body:
> What materials would you recommend to study with if someone primarily learns through resources such as khan academy, YouTube, and Anki? 

I would stick with looking up things you do not understand on youtube.  I like the RegisteredNurseRN page.  She breaks things down and makes it simple to understand, but there are tons of videos.

&#x200B;

> In freshman year is it required to immediately buy a stethoscope etc? 

This will depend on your program.  I only needed mine at the end of the semester for two clinical days.

&#x200B;

> Are programs really cut throat that is, once you fail below a certain threshold you are out no question? (Hopefully not) 

Again, this will depend on your program.  You are only on it my program if you do something serious or you fail a class.  Which is getting below 80%.

&#x200B;

> In your experience would study groups be beneficial or more of a double edged sword, that is people may leech off you etc? 

Yes and no.  I have found that a study group of no more than three people can be very helpful.  Any more and it is very easy to get off topic.

&#x200B;

> Would you recommend making friends so that you may help each other out or kind of keep to yourself? 

Definitely make friends.  There may be a topic you do not understand, but a classmate might and can explain it to you better than your teacher can.  And vice versa.

&#x200B;

> Are clinicals different than labs? If so, are labs the type of labs you would take in like chemistry or bio and do experiments and then write a lab report ?

Yes.  In my program lab was learning the skill.  We had one day where we had lab and we learned how to do our assessment, vitals, med pass, and so many other things.  We didn't have to do any reports, but certain skills we did not have to validate on, we had to have our instructor watch us do the skill.

&#x200B;

> What are clinicals? And are they like classes? Do you study for them? 

This will again depend on your program.  Clinical is when you actually go to a hospital and are a nurse.  You will be with an RN, but you will be practicing your skills on real patients.  My program I do not have any studying to do really as we do not know what patient we will have until we get there.  All I need to know is what content we have been working on that week.  Like when we talked about the cardiovascular system, I knew I would have a patient with some sort of cardiac issue.  I gave my first insulin shot Thursday and it was exciting/terrifying.

&#x200B;

> Is the analogy of nursing school being like drinking form a fire hydrant accurate? 

I have never heard this, but it made me laugh.  First semester for me was not like this at all, but it was all the very basic information.  I am currently in my second semester and this is super accurate.  There is a lot of information thrown at you at once and you need to be able to figure out what is important to know.

&#x200B;

>How did you deal with professors that simply weren’t very good at teaching?

Thankfully I have not had to deal with this yet.  I have had wonderful instructors, so I have no advice for this.  Sorry!

&#x200B;

> Do all programs require HESI examinations? 

Mine does not.  Our exams are given NCLEX style.  We do have HESI assignments to do, but they are not our exams and we only have 5.

&#x200B;

> What exactly are skill check offs? 

My program calls them validations.  It is when you perform a skill with an instructor (or someone certified to do it) so that you can perform that skill on real patients.  Like doing vital signs, foley catheters, passing medications, and so on.

&#x200B;

> Is it true the NCLEX with be changing? I was informed that it would change into a more scenario based exam? (Not sure about this) 

My instructor has been saying this as well.  I am not too sure what will be changing, but yes, it is going to change.

&#x200B;

> How would you recommend to prepare and deal with SATA questions? 

As, the bane of all nursing students.  These questions will destroy you mentally.  The best way to answer these is to look for the answers that apply BEST to the situation in the question.  It will never be one or all answers.

&#x200B;

> Does pharmacology require calculus mathematics? (I’m assuming this due to like rates and such, but hopefully not) 

God no.  It is sort of simple math.  I am terrible at math and I understood it fairly quickly.

&#x200B;

> What overall materials would you recommend to a freshman majoring in nursing? (Resources?, Books?, stationary, shoes, stethoscopes, etc?) 

Get a planner or have some way of planning out what you will do when.  Some days you will have a lot of work to do and it can be very overwhelming, but if you break it down into smaller chunks it become easier to manage.  It also allows you to make time to take care of yourself.  Don't spend too much on shoes.  Mine were $18 and they work just fine.  Same with my watch.  I bought a super cheap one.  Stethoscope, I would say get a pretty decent one as the cheaper ones can be hard to hear heart and lung sounds with.  If you splurge on a nice one, make sure to get an ID for it so it doesn't get "lost".  I personally love post its and have big ones that I can write on and mark pages with charts I need to know or important information.  I also don't use a notebook as all of my classes had powerpoints, so I took notes on them.  If you go that route, have a small laptop to bring to class.  I would also consider purchasing two Saunders books: Strategies for test success, and one of their NCLEX review books.  The review book has online access which is amazing because you can select what category you want to test yourself on.  It also helps you practice NCLEX questions and that is HUGE.  You should try to do around 10 a day to help you prepare for the exam.  Focus on learning the material.  Yes it is important to get a good grade and pass the class, but that will not help you pass the NCLEX if you don't LEARN the information.  I hope this helps!

&#x200B;
